# deploy/Caddyfile -- edge for the VPS production stack (tracker dist:RP4, plan D14 / D15(c) /
# section 6 answer 4). Identical in substance to src/collector/Caddyfile (RP2) -- same
# self-signed-cert-until-a-domain-exists reasoning, same edge body-size cap, same reverse proxy
# target -- kept as its own file here (rather than a symlink or an include) because deploy.yml
# syncs exactly two files (docker-compose.yml, Caddyfile) to the VPS and a symlink does not
# survive `scp`. If you change one of the two Caddyfiles, change both; tools/lint_compose.py does
# not diff them (a Caddyfile is not YAML), so this stays a human discipline, not a gate.
#
# No domain exists yet, so this serves a SELF-SIGNED certificate via Caddy's `tls internal`
# directive (its own local CA, generated on first run) rather than attempting public ACME
# issuance, which would fail with no DNS name to validate. The launcher (dist RP1) is expected to
# pin a public key rather than trust a CA -- and it must be the LOCAL ROOT CA's key, not the
# leaf's: `tls internal` re-issues short-lived leaves (and re-keys them), so a leaf SPKI pin
# breaks on the first rotation. Print the root once the stack is up with:
#   docker compose exec caddy caddy trust  # or: openssl s_client -connect <host>:443 </dev/null 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -pubkey -noout | openssl pkey -pubin -outform der | openssl dgst -sha256 -binary | base64
# Revisit once a domain exists: swap `tls internal` for a bare site address and Caddy's automatic
# HTTPS (Let's Encrypt) takes over with no other change.

# THE SITE ADDRESS IS AN ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE, AND `default_sni` IS LOAD-BEARING (first live deploy,
# 2026-09-18). A bare `:443` site names no host, so `tls internal` has nothing to issue a
# certificate FOR: the listener came up with zero certificates and every TLS handshake died with
# `alert internal error` (curl: HTTP 000, the deploy's /healthz poll never passed). The address
# has to be the name or IP clients dial, and this file may not carry the VPS's IP (the repo's
# machine-path lint refuses it; the value lives in the GitHub Environment secret VPS_HOST), so it
# arrives as MH_SITE_ADDR -- deploy.yml writes it into the VPS's .env, docker-compose.yml hands it
# to this container. Unset (the local dev compose) it falls back to `localhost`.
# `default_sni` matters on its own: a client that dials an IP LITERAL sends no SNI at all (RFC 6066
# forbids it), and without a default name Caddy has no way to choose the certificate even once one
# exists. The launcher (dist RP1) and the deploy's own curl both dial the IP.
{
	default_sni {$MH_SITE_ADDR:localhost}
}

{$MH_SITE_ADDR:localhost} {
	tls internal

	# The edge body-size cap (plan D14): a request over 64 MB is rejected with 413 HERE, before
	# Caddy even finishes proxying it to the collector process. The collector also enforces the
	# same limit itself (collector/middleware.py) so it is not solely dependent on this front door.
	request_body {
		max_size 64MB
	}

	reverse_proxy collector:8000 {
		# Give large uploads (up to 64 MB) room to actually transfer.
		transport http {
			read_timeout 120s
			write_timeout 120s
		}
	}

	log {
		output stdout
		format json
	}
}

:80 {
	redir https://{host}{uri} permanent
}
